System Description
The intent of the "Unstable Bluff" Detection System is to monitor unstable bluffs in the Del Mar, California, area 
and identify changes that may indicate a potential bluff collapse. The prototype system will use six stationary Wi-Fi-enabled cameras 
to monitor a 300-foot area of bluff. Each camera will periodically capture images of a 50-foot section of the bluff and transmit the
images to a central processing system. The central processing system will store and compare images to detect changes in the bluff.
Each change will be rated from zero to five depending on the amount of change. A rating of zero indicates no identifiable changes. Rating of 1 and 3
are considered predictive of a significant slide. A rating of four denotes a significant shift, and additional assessment is needed right away
to decide whether rail operations should be halted and people evacuated from the area. A rating of five indicates rail traffic should be stopped,
immediately, and people evacuated from the area to avoid injury or death. In addition, the images indicate major changes should be compared to the last 
image from that location to determine whether trains or people were in the area before the change occurred. When the rating is between 4 and 5, the system
will generate an alert and send notifications to the rail operators and lifeguards.
